+### W3.1 — PPSSPP libretro Vulkan contract & geometry (source: `D:\Arcade\build\ppsspp`)
+- **Negotiation = functionally v1, but the core reports version 2.** `LibretroVulkanContext.cpp:98-105` initializes the negotiation struct with only 5 of 7 fields → `create_instance` and `create_device2` are **NULL**, while `..._VERSION` is `2` (`libretro_vulkan.h:30`). Per the header contract (`:196`), a NULL `create_device2` means fall back to v1 `create_device`. **Our selector already handles this correctly:** `nanoarch_vulkan.c:499` and `:592` gate the v2 paths on `interface_version >= 2 && vk.negotiation.create_instance`/`create_device2` (the callback-pointer AND-term), so a version-2/NULL-callback core correctly falls through to the v1 `create_device` branch (`:612`). The audit's "single biggest risk" (a selector branching purely on `version >= 2`) does **not** apply to our code.
+- **Device features/extensions merge cleanly.** PPSSPP enables features as "if-supported" (`VulkanContext.cpp:742-750` — none hard-required; no `wideLines`) and the libretro warp `libretro_vulkan.cpp:101-103,135-140` **appends** the frontend's `required_device_extensions` and OR's the `required_features` — so our injected `VK_KHR_external_memory_win32` + `VK_KHR_external_semaphore_win32` (v1 zero-copy path, `:612-660`) are added on top, not clobbered.
+- **Geometry:** base = `iInternalResolution × 480×272` (`libretro.cpp:1322-1325`); the delivered hw-render frame IS that full size (the psp config's 960×544 is the worker's post-capture downscale, not core output). Static per session; changes only on a deliberate `ppsspp_internal_resolution` option change (`libretro.cpp:1100-1114`) — which our zc caps-reneg already handles. FMV/display-mode switches do NOT change base geometry.
+- **⚠ LIKELY BLOCKER — headless surface.** `create_device` (`LibretroVulkanContext.cpp:39-79`) calls `vk->InitSurface(WINDOWSYSTEM_WIN32,...)` then (in `CreateDrawContext`) `InitSwapchain`. `vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR` is warped to return the frontend's surface handle (`libretro_vulkan.cpp:152-155`), and `vkGetPhysicalDeviceSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR_libretro` passes it to the **real driver** `_org` (`:157-158`). We pass `VK_NULL_HANDLE` as the surface (`nanoarch_vulkan.c:593,636`), so that real query is `vkGetPhysicalDeviceSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR(gpu, VK_NULL_HANDLE)` — invalid; on failure PPSSPP's `InitSwapchain` returns false → no draw context → **no video**. (The `assert(surface)` at `libretro_vulkan.cpp:441` is likely NDEBUG-stripped in the release DLL, but the functional use of the handle is the real problem.) This extends the W2 addendum's own deferred "if it later wants `VK_KHR_swapchain` that is a follow-up with fresh evidence." **This is the #1 psp-Vk boot-probe risk.** Remedy if the probe confirms it: the frontend synthesizes a real headless `VkSurfaceKHR` (message-only HWND + `vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR`; the instance already carries `VK_KHR_surface`+`VK_KHR_win32_surface` from `4451d47`, add `VK_KHR_swapchain` to the device) and passes it into `create_device` — a **scoped code follow-up**, deliberately NOT done here (it can't be validated compile-only and would be an unvalidated change to the proven paraLLEl-GS/N64 `create_device` call site).
+
+**Bottom line:** wiring + merge + geometry are all compatible; PPSSPP-Vk is likely one surface-handle fix short of booting. Gate on the boot probe (§4). → **CONFIRMED LIVE, then FIXED — see §W3.1-LIVE.**
+
+### W3.1-LIVE — the blocker confirmed one step deeper, and the fix (commit `0baec66`, `worker.w3fix.exe`)
+**Live crash (glworker.log 2026-07-16 15:06:16, room `…-psp___Daxter`):**
+```
+[vk] negotiation interface stored (version 2, create_instance=no create_device2=no)   → v1 path
+[vk] instance created by frontend (v1 path, 2 surface extension(s) enabled)            → 4451d47 worked
+PPSSPP: instance fns loaded, chose the 4070 Ti, "Vulkan Device created",
+        "Setting Vulkan availability to true"                                          → create_device SUCCEEDED with our VK_NULL_HANDLE surface
+[G3D] Creating Vulkan surface for window (data1=0000… data2=0000…)  → worker EXIT 0xC0000005
+```
+So the audit's surface-caps-query concern did NOT kill create_device — the crash is **one step later**: PPSSPP proceeds (at `CreateDrawContext`) to build its OWN Win32 surface via `VulkanContext::ReinitSurface` (`VulkanContext.cpp:951-971`). That `vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR` is **warped** — `LOAD_INSTANCE_FUNC(vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR)` (`VulkanLoader.cpp:720`) runs during `CreateInstance`, AFTER `vk_libretro_init` installs the warp, so it resolves to `vkCreateLibretroSurfaceKHR` (`libretro_vulkan.cpp:152-155`) which **echoes `vk_init_info.surface` back verbatim, ignoring the null window data**. `vk_init_info.surface` = the handle the frontend passed into `create_device` = `VK_NULL_HANDLE`. PPSSPP then uses that null handle for its swapchain/surface-caps path and access-violates. `data1=data2=0` in the log is the hardcoded `InitSurface(WIN32, nullptr, nullptr)` (`LibretroVulkanContext.cpp:54`) — but the warp means those window fields are irrelevant; the **surface handle** is what matters.
+
+**Answer to task #1 (does PPSSPP skip its own surface if we pass a real one?):** No — `create_device` unconditionally calls `InitSurface(WIN32, null, null)` and `ReinitSurface` regardless. But it does NOT need to skip: `vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR` is warped to echo `vk_init_info.surface`, so handing a REAL surface into `create_device` makes PPSSPP's "own" surface BE our real surface. The null window data is never used by the warp.
+
+**Answer to task #4 (swapchain vs libretro frame handoff):** frames still arrive through the libretro image interface, NOT a real present. `vkCreateSwapchainKHR` is warped (`libretro_vulkan.cpp:199-260`) to allocate a ring of plain `VkImage`s (dedicated memory) and hand them to the frontend via `retro_vulkan_set_image` on the negotiation interface — there is no real `VkSwapchainKHR`, no present queue, no `vkQueuePresentKHR` to the surface. The surface exists only to satisfy PPSSPP's `InitSwapchain` surface-caps/format queries; our capture path (M1 readback / M2 zero-copy) is unchanged. So the re-probe should still show `zero-copy: ACTIVE` (or `readback path`), never a present error.
+
+**The fix (`nanoarch_vulkan.c`, gated by hack `vk_synthetic_surface`):** between `pick_physical_device` and `create_device`, `create_synthetic_surface()` registers a hidden `WS_POPUP` window (real `HWND` via `CreateWindowExW`, never shown — a message-only window can't back a Vulkan surface) and creates a real `VkSurfaceKHR` on our instance via `vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR` (the v1 headless instance already carries `VK_KHR_surface` + `VK_KHR_win32_surface` from `4451d47`). That surface is passed into `create_device` / `create_device2` in place of `VK_NULL_HANDLE`; via the warp it becomes PPSSPP's surface. Torn down in `nano_vk_shutdown` (surface destroyed before the instance, window after). Plumbed exactly like `skip_hw_context_destroy`: `meta.HasHack("vk_synthetic_surface")` → `Nan0.hackVkSyntheticSurface` → `armVulkanSyntheticSurface()` (a flag write before `nano_vk_init`, alongside `armVulkanZeroCopy`). **Inert for ps2/n64** — they don't set the hack, so `vk.synth_surface` stays `VK_NULL_HANDLE` and their proven paths are byte-for-byte unchanged (re-confirmed live on `worker.w3.exe` before this build).
+
+**Re-probe (must show):** on `worker.w3fix.exe` + psp arm (with `vk_synthetic_surface` in psp `hacks`): `synthetic headless surface created (hidden WS_POPUP window)`, `device created by core (v1 create_device)`, `hw render context: Vulkan`, `[vk] zero-copy: ACTIVE` (or `readback path`), 60 t/s / forgiven=0, NO 0xC0000005 after "Creating Vulkan surface for window", LocoRoco+Daxter render, memstick seed+harvest intact, close ×5 clean (surface+window freed each close). Then the crackle A/B (Eric remote; worker-side pace-diag II only).
+
+### W4.1 — flycast libretro Vulkan contract & geometry (source: DLL strings — flycast tree not checked out; `flycast_libretro.dll`, git `324d9c1`)
+- **Vulkan renderer + OIT present:** `vulkan_renderer.cpp`/`VulkanRenderer::Init`, `oit/oit_renderer.cpp`/`OITVulkanRenderer::Init`, `retro_vk_context_reset`/`_destroy`, `Get Vulkan HW interface failed` — all strings in the DLL. So the core WILL run Vulkan **iff the worker negotiates a Vulkan hw-context**.
+- **⚠ NO renderer core-option.** There is **no `reicast_renderer`** (only `reicast_threaded_rendering`); no `Vulkan`/`OpenGL`/`Auto` value cluster. flycast picks the renderer from the frontend's hw-context, not a core option. **Consequence:** W4 dc-Vulkan is NOT a config renderer flip — `hwContext:"vulkan"` (the F1 field / core-config key) is the ONLY mechanism, and a per-game GL escape must use `hwContext:"gl"` (F1), not an option. A `reicast_renderer:` line would be a silently-ignored unknown key.
+- **OIT tokens (verbatim from the DLL option strings, VALUES not labels):** `reicast_alpha_sorting` ∈ {`per-strip (fast, least accurate)` | `per-triangle (normal)` | `per-pixel (accurate)`}; `reicast_oit_abuffer_size` ∈ {`512MB` | `1GB` | `2GB` | `4GB`} — **no space** (the spaced "512 MB" forms are GUI translations). The spec's guesses `"per-pixel (accurate)"` and `"512MB"` are both correct.
+- **Geometry:** `retro_get_system_av_info` is renderer-agnostic (one code path; base = DC native 640×480, internal-res multiplier → max_width) — so the Vk renderer hands frames the same way GL does and **dc `scale:2` should hold** (640×480 × 2 = 1280×960 passthrough). This is INFERRED (source not checked out); the gated probe MUST read the `retro_get_system_av_info: Res=` / `System A/V >>>` line under the Vk renderer — if base comes back as internal res, re-derive `scale` to 1. Also: per-pixel OIT needs GL≥4.3 **or** Vulkan, so it is technically GL-shippable — but the point is the Vk headroom, so it rides the Vulkan arm.
+
+**Bottom line:** dc-Vulkan is achievable with the shipped DLL, via `hwContext:"vulkan"` (not a renderer option) + the two verified OIT tokens; scale stays 2 pending the live geometry line.
